{"title":"New York City fact and poetic passage of the day","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/912629477/0/marginalrevolution~New-York-City-fact-and-poetic-passage-of-the-day.html","date":1739131316000,"content":"<blockquote><p>If the coastline of the New York Harbor region were stretched out, it would be longer than the state of California.  New York City’s waterfront is bigger than those of Miami, San Francisco, Los Angeles, and Boston combined.  As vast as it is, the area that is officially known as the New York-New Jersey Harbor Estuary is even more staggering in its complexity, encompassing such a concatenation of inlets, margins, banks, strands, runnels, rivers, reefs, rivulets, coves, creeks, and kills; of brooks, basins, bays, shoals, shores, islands, islets, and peninsulas, of jetties, bluffs, heights, scallops, spits, crags, beaches, reaches, bends, bights, channels, sandbars, sounds, and points, as to be virtually unmatched in the United States.</p></blockquote>\r\n<p>That is from the new and fun book by Russell Shorto, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Taking-Manhattan-Extraordinary-Created-America/dp/0393881164/ref=sr_1_1?crid=39PLIX1L46DQD&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.qkPXSHVDAJ-qFuU1e5X3iYn8fhcee_k4aqTqd8tQS9UG2WwtXeKuHizGL5SqDo4pbgDVRJrC0p5Pl-qEnOXK7upymsl2jMD7Lb63bK_ZTmhgCkv--v3Y7hE2DFxuBr4UE6pXte7aLRQx9cPtC1ITBM2DqKeeHzf4B8rVwLnlNQr4f4-OAZ7AjU9IQCy2cxXMwV_xs7BRCoc8ryZ0CrPOlpMeLms4iW3V-pNL7uLz4kCa1wVAT6LgcHI5yPS6poibJsumZnmxXDAazcMo86fYkxYnUNg3oPND8K7TPe-SNkIkxMtj_oIgmM2lg0fSf2gTaJwiO9MCiTCCqFuoIcUZ3buTOBmGj32ElpP6Ym-H5KdCDQ2LkzFgILOU8n7PskjlZ1whl10b-2Qnv8UUJVbcg2medgLuxmABqwFTDKfnCTiGv6viUpTvkog54D0NTJqL.VZbtUk0LEMjRluRDVw5RsgjydVGt7jlmuS7Od0YWo7M&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=russell+shorto&amp;qid=1738962122&amp;sprefix=russell+shorto%2Caps%2C94&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">Taking Manhattan: The Extraordinary Events that Created New York and Shaped America</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https:/